WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:05.860
40 years ago, a U.S. Navy exploratory vessel
landed on a chain of islands in the South

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:09.559
Atlantic, just 2,000 miles off the southern
coast of Argentina.

00:00:09.559 --> 00:00:12.570
In other words, the middle of nowhere.

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:17.250
Yet on this far-off set of rocks afloat in
the middle of the ocean, they discovered a

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:23.449
vast array of strange wildlife and plant species
that resemble little of what they left behind

00:00:23.449 --> 00:00:24.449
on the mainland.

00:00:24.449 --> 00:00:28.689
Could this be a treasure trove of undiscovered
species, they wondered?

00:00:28.689 --> 00:00:33.520
Hoping to capitalize on the discovery, a reconnaissance
team of researchers was sent out to explore

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:35.060
some of the islands on foot.

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:37.430
And what they found did not disappoint.

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:42.480
From endangered species of birds, to plant
life and vegetation that looked like it belonged

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:48.850
on Mars, they found a dizzying array of new
and exciting examples of evolutionary oddities

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:50.800
that would fill a library.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:54.890
But unfortunately, their jubilation was cut
short.

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:59.670
Attempting to collect soil samples, they dug
into the ground and soon discovered that what

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:02.730
they were standing on wasn’t actually ground
at all.

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:07.300
The navy scientists soon discovered the rocky
terrain they were exploring was actually made

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:10.680
up entirely of organic matter.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:15.770
As per the standard protocol, the SCP Foundation
was forced to silence the U.S. Navy research

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:17.810
vessel after its discovery.

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:21.900
With the okay from the United States government,
the ship was torpedoed and sunk to the bottom

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:27.530
of the ocean with all hands on deck, in the
name of protecting the greater good of the

00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:28.530
masses.

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:31.880
A tragedy at sea for the sake of preventing
worldwide pandemonium.

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:36.310
From there the Foundation took over with the
containment procedures of what would eventually

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:43.120
come to be known as SCP-169, better known
by its nickname, the Leviathan.

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:48.490
The Leviathan is a biblical creature of mythic
proportions, said to be able to boil the seas

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:53.600
and create Earth-ending floods with just a
whip of its massive continent-spanning body.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:02:00.030
It’s unclear if SCP-169 has the ability
to do these things, though, given its massive

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:05.850
size, which Foundation researchers estimate
to be somewhere between 2000 and 8000 kilometers,

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:11.129
it’s easy to see how something that big
moving at any speed could be devastating for

00:02:11.129 --> 00:02:13.499
the coastal regions of the world.

00:02:13.499 --> 00:02:18.139
Although we use the word “contain”, it
is impossible for SCP staff to ever contain

00:02:18.139 --> 00:02:20.829
something of such massive size.

00:02:20.829 --> 00:02:24.269
Surveillance and monitoring are about all
we can do for now.

00:02:24.269 --> 00:02:28.159
That and hope it never wakes up and decides
that we’re on the menu.

00:02:28.159 --> 00:02:34.200
It was all thought to be relatively smooth
sailing until one incident changed everything…

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:39.790
Dr. Hart turned off the video monitor and
faced the assembled members of MTF Gamma-6,

00:02:39.790 --> 00:02:44.950
also known as the Deep Feeders, renowned for
their specialization in the tracking of deep-sea

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:46.400
and oceanic anomalies.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:52.010
The room was dark, and each team member was
sitting in shadow, but still, Dr. Hart could

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:54.549
see the seriousness on their faces.

00:02:54.549 --> 00:02:58.760
“I’m sure you're all wondering why I called
you here at such short notice,'' he said.

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:04.139
On a screen behind him, he put up a NASA satellite
picture of a small set of rocky islands.

00:03:04.139 --> 00:03:06.330
“You see this little island chain here?

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:11.269
Well, it wasn’t there a week ago.”, he
paused, rubbing his forehead from lack of

00:03:11.269 --> 00:03:12.269
sleep.

00:03:12.269 --> 00:03:18.249
“At 8:05 am today, we recorded another auditory
anomaly emitting from SCP-169.

00:03:18.249 --> 00:03:23.340
This one was much louder and longer than the
one recorded in 1997 by the National Oceanic

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:24.340
and Atmospheric Administration.

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:27.360
The one known as “the Bloop”.

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:31.719
Back then it was easy to brush it off as some
ice breaking off of a continental shelf and

00:03:31.719 --> 00:03:33.290
scraping against the seafloor.

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:36.219
It’s not so easy to cover it up anymore.

00:03:36.219 --> 00:03:41.040
These auditory emissions from SCP-169 are
beginning to grow more frequent.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:45.829
More than that, increased seismic activity
around the archipelago on the creature’s

00:03:45.829 --> 00:03:49.989
back indicates that the rate at which the
creature is breathing is also up.

00:03:49.989 --> 00:03:54.489
Typically the rate at which the creature takes
what we are assuming to be a “breath”

00:03:54.489 --> 00:03:56.099
is once every three months.

00:03:56.099 --> 00:03:58.930
That rate has increased to once every three
weeks.

00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:04.530
For as long as we’ve been monitoring SCP-169,
it’s been adrift along the southern Atlantic,

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:07.260
never moving more than a kilometer a week.

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:10.379
That behavior is slowly beginning to change.

00:04:10.379 --> 00:04:15.439
It doesn’t sound like much, but something
that big even moving just a fraction of that

00:04:15.439 --> 00:04:21.390
faster means 100-foot tidal waves for the
entire South American coastline.

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:24.370
We fear the creature is showing signs of waking
up.

00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:29.909
If this is true, it could mean a cataclysm
for hundreds of nations around the world.

00:04:29.909 --> 00:04:34.090
Millions, hell even billions could be wiped
off the face of the Earth if this thing just

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:36.419
flips its body too fast in the wrong direction.

00:04:36.419 --> 00:04:40.710
To put it to scale, this monster is about
the size of the Caribbean, maybe even bigger

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:41.710
as far as we know.”

00:04:41.710 --> 00:04:47.300
The doctor then pulled open a decanter of
whiskey and poured himself a glass, swallowing

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:55.110
the entire pour in one go, in an appalling
breach of workplace professionalism.

00:04:55.110 --> 00:05:01.509
“We’re talking about an extinction-level
event.

00:05:01.509 --> 00:05:05.050
A bonafide XK-Class End of the World Scenario.

00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:09.580
1,000 ft tsunami waves stretching as far as
the eye can see from either end of the horizon.

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:13.360
The last time SCP-169 stirred, it created
the Mediterranean.

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:18.879
In fact, the legends of the biblical flood,
Noah’s arc, the same story can be found

00:05:18.879 --> 00:05:23.419
in every civilization and culture around the
world, so maybe they were on to something.

00:05:23.419 --> 00:05:25.689
It is to this end I call you all here.

00:05:25.689 --> 00:05:28.069
To save the world from the next great flood.”

00:05:28.069 --> 00:05:32.539
He paused for a moment as the room took in
the gravity of the situation.

00:05:32.539 --> 00:05:38.340
“The more we can learn about SCP-169 the
better, and right now we know squat.

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:42.720
For one, we’ve never seen just how deep
the body of the creature goes, or what’s

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:43.720
under it.

00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:47.069
The pressure has been too great for our current
model submersibles.

00:05:47.069 --> 00:05:48.569
Until now.

00:05:48.569 --> 00:05:53.780
After reinforcing our Nemo-1 deep-sea submersible
with thaumatological runes, the integrity

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:56.669
of its hull has increased exponentially.

00:05:56.669 --> 00:06:01.449
Your mission will be to travel as far down
and as close to the creature as possible.

00:06:01.449 --> 00:06:04.780
If it breathes, then it must have gills or
lungs.

00:06:04.780 --> 00:06:09.389
There must be some basic biological facts
we need to understand if we’re going to

00:06:09.389 --> 00:06:11.330
survive extinction.

00:06:11.330 --> 00:06:15.000
Our only recourse is to find a way to sedate
the creature.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:18.099
And if that doesn’t work, then…

00:06:18.099 --> 00:06:19.740
God help us all...”

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:24.539
Just 12 hours later, deep in the waters of
the Southern Atlantic, a massive submersible

00:06:24.539 --> 00:06:28.099
ship was diving down into the depths of the
dark ocean.

00:06:28.099 --> 00:06:31.090
Looking for a way to stop the end of the world.

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:36.620
Agent Ɣ6-1, a member of Deep Feeders, looked
out of the massive viewing port as the ship

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:43.270
slowly descended alongside the rocky trench
that made up the body of SCP-169.

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:47.669
Behind him was Dr. Hart, sporting a white
5-o'clock shadow and a red nose from a night

00:06:47.669 --> 00:06:49.969
of drinking to calm his nerves.

00:06:49.969 --> 00:06:54.449
The entire crew were in silent awe as the
giant floodlights of the ship brushed over

00:06:54.449 --> 00:06:58.199
the barnacle-covered surfaces of the undersea
mountain.

00:06:58.199 --> 00:07:02.949
They progressed slowly, breaching each milestone
in depth with caution, knowing that one wrong

00:07:02.949 --> 00:07:06.120
move could destabilize the pressure in their
ship.

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:08.430
Ɣ6-1 murmured the word, “Scales.”

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:10.389
“What?” asked Dr. Hart.

00:07:10.389 --> 00:07:13.740
“I’ve been watching the patterns of the
ridges as we sink down.

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:15.360
They look like scales.

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:16.360
Giant segments.

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:19.449
It’s hard to see at first because they’re
so covered in barnacles and sediment but,

00:07:19.449 --> 00:07:20.560
I’m sure of it.

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:25.919
They’re giant armored scales, the way lobsters
or crabs have armored exoskeletons.”

00:07:25.919 --> 00:07:29.300
“Interesting,” the doctor said, scratching
his chin.

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:32.169
“Arthropods have segmented armored bodies.

00:07:32.169 --> 00:07:36.249
This means we might be looking at some sort
of evolutionary hybrid.

00:07:36.249 --> 00:07:40.699
Some sort of aquatic mammal such as a whale
or dolphin, but one that has developed armored

00:07:40.699 --> 00:07:43.539
segmented scales similar to that of a crustacean.”

00:07:43.539 --> 00:07:46.030
The doctor crossed his arms to think.

00:07:46.030 --> 00:07:50.289
“The closest thing we have on the fossil
record of this magnitude is an armored fish

00:07:50.289 --> 00:07:54.729
from the Cretaceous period, but even that’s
way too small to compare to this.

00:07:54.729 --> 00:07:58.199
This thing has entire ecosystems on its back.”

00:07:58.199 --> 00:08:00.110
“How old is it?” asked the agent.

00:08:00.110 --> 00:08:04.749
“Well now that you mention it, given the
impossible age of the creature, an arthropod

00:08:04.749 --> 00:08:06.650
would make sense…

00:08:06.650 --> 00:08:10.060
Lobsters and other crustaceans don’t age
conventionally, you see.

00:08:10.060 --> 00:08:11.330
They are effectively immortal.

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:16.180
In fact, they actually die because they continue
to grow until their bodies can’t hold up

00:08:16.180 --> 00:08:18.680
their humongous size anymore.

00:08:18.680 --> 00:08:22.810
And yet SCP-169 just kept growing...”

00:08:22.810 --> 00:08:24.970
the doctor trailed off, thinking to himself.

00:08:24.970 --> 00:08:30.469
Dr. Hart, still lost in thought, turned to
walk into the back of the submersible as Ɣ6-1

00:08:30.469 --> 00:08:31.469
followed.

00:08:31.469 --> 00:08:36.110
He said, as much to himself as to the Agent,
“The creature is old.

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:41.390
The geological survey conducted when SCP-169
was first discovered carbon-dated the specimen

00:08:41.390 --> 00:08:44.160
to over 541 million years old.

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:47.550
As you can probably surmise, that’s too
old for any living being.

00:08:47.550 --> 00:08:52.760
The results had to be incorrect, and yet,
I checked them myself more than once.

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:54.490
And numbers do not lie.

00:08:54.490 --> 00:08:56.640
This entity predates our civilization.

00:08:56.640 --> 00:09:00.850
Hell, it predates complex life forms on this
planet as we know it.

00:09:00.850 --> 00:09:04.650
From an evolutionary standpoint, it doesn’t
make any sense.”

00:09:04.650 --> 00:09:09.440
The Agent, confused, asked, “But doctor,
how could something so big even come into

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:10.440
existence?”

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:12.520
“Very little of our planet is actually landmass.

00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:15.000
It’s a wonder we call it Earth.

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:17.150
Most of this planet is water.

00:09:17.150 --> 00:09:21.200
We aren’t looking at a creature that’s
on our planet, agent.

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:23.700
We are on its planet.”

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:27.660
Just then a flurry of alarms and sensors began
to go off at the bridge of the ship.

00:09:27.660 --> 00:09:31.450
The team scrambled to their stations checking
off monitors and shouting readings to each

00:09:31.450 --> 00:09:32.450
other.

00:09:32.450 --> 00:09:34.950
“The ship's thermal sensors are detecting
a massive rise in temperature, transmitting

00:09:34.950 --> 00:09:36.380
power to cooling systems.”

00:09:36.380 --> 00:09:38.620
“We have activity northwest of our location.

00:09:38.620 --> 00:09:41.640
Sonar has something big headed our way, we
need to dive NOW!”

00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:46.720
The ship lurched forward, nose first, as everyone
onboard held on to their seats, their restraints

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:49.000
keeping them from falling forward.

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:53.700
Just outside of the viewing port, Dr. Hart
could see an enormous scaled appendage moving

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:55.180
fast in their direction.

00:09:55.180 --> 00:09:58.100
“We need to steer the ship clear of the
talons!

00:09:58.100 --> 00:09:59.660
Revert all power to the thrusters!

00:09:59.660 --> 00:10:00.910
Get us out of the way!”

00:10:00.910 --> 00:10:01.910
the doctor yelled.

00:10:01.910 --> 00:10:03.800
“Don’t you think I’m doing that!”

00:10:03.800 --> 00:10:05.230
the captain shouted back.

00:10:05.230 --> 00:10:09.650
The ship jolted sideways and downwards as
an immense wave of pressure came over the

00:10:09.650 --> 00:10:13.980
submersible, putting 6 G’s of force on its
occupants.

00:10:13.980 --> 00:10:18.520
Ɣ6-1 yelped out of fear as the ship came
close to crashing into what appeared to be

00:10:18.520 --> 00:10:23.430
the edge of a titanic talon the size of the
empire state building.

00:10:23.430 --> 00:10:27.180
Following the encounter, the part of the ship
bordering the reinforced glass of the viewing

00:10:27.180 --> 00:10:29.530
port began to glow bright red.

00:10:29.530 --> 00:10:32.760
“It’s boiling the water around us!”
one of the crewmembers cried out.

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:37.210
“It’s not boiling anything, it's just
cavitation due to the pressure reduction in

00:10:37.210 --> 00:10:38.210
the water.

00:10:38.210 --> 00:10:42.480
The updated runes on the ship’s hull should
keep us safe, we just need to cool our heads.

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:44.820
Stay focused on the mission, bottom feeders.”

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:49.420
After several moments of holding their breaths,
the crew of the Nemo-1 began to relax as their

00:10:49.420 --> 00:10:53.680
pace returned to a slow descent along the
side of the underwater behemoth.

00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:59.100
Before long, the vessel was no longer traveling
straight down, but starting to curve under

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:05.020
the creature, traveling north towards the
theoretical head of SCP-169.

00:11:05.020 --> 00:11:10.890
After several hours, the ship came upon giant
fissures in the rocky exterior of the creature.

00:11:10.890 --> 00:11:15.890
These massive vent-like structures appeared
stiff but slowly they opened and closed over

00:11:15.890 --> 00:11:17.950
a span of weeks.

00:11:17.950 --> 00:11:20.990
These were the respiratory organs of the organism.

00:11:20.990 --> 00:11:25.480
Clocking in at over 2 miles underneath the
surface of the ocean, the crew carefully began

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:30.220
entering the smaller pod-like submersibles
that detached from the main ship.

00:11:30.220 --> 00:11:34.890
Ɣ6-1 entered his pod and strapped on the
haptic gloves that would give him control

00:11:34.890 --> 00:11:36.590
of the pod’s robotic arms.

00:11:36.590 --> 00:11:42.130
He and a team of 3 other volunteers had agreed
to undergo the dangerous mission of attaching

00:11:42.130 --> 00:11:46.290
artificial chemical emission machines that
could be programmed to release anesthetic

00:11:46.290 --> 00:11:51.680
gas into SCP-169’s respiratory system on
command remotely.

00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:55.800
The emission machines could be refueled manually
whenever the contents ran out.

00:11:55.800 --> 00:12:00.480
It was an ingenious solution Dr. Hart came
up with when thinking of his time studying

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:02.500
sharks as a marine biologist.

00:12:02.500 --> 00:12:07.380
They would tag the fins of sharks by capturing
them and bringing the specimens on the deck

00:12:07.380 --> 00:12:08.620
of their boat.

00:12:08.620 --> 00:12:13.050
From there the scientists would drill a radio
frequency emitter onto the fin of the shark.

00:12:13.050 --> 00:12:17.170
When he had first seen this practice as a
young college student, Dr. Hart was afraid

00:12:17.170 --> 00:12:19.130
the process was harming the shark.

00:12:19.130 --> 00:12:23.450
But later he learned it was designed in a
way to not be harmful to the specimen and

00:12:23.450 --> 00:12:28.620
eventually the tag would fall off after enough
data on the shark’s movement was collected.

00:12:28.620 --> 00:12:31.680
Except these tags would not be falling off,
he thought.

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:32.870
They could not.

00:12:32.870 --> 00:12:36.240
For the sake of all mankind, this had to work.

00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:39.680
The doctor pressed a button on the receiver
of the radio and spoke.

00:12:39.680 --> 00:12:41.560
“Ɣ6-1, what is your status?”

00:12:41.560 --> 00:12:42.560
“Pressure is holding.

00:12:42.560 --> 00:12:43.850
All signs look good.”

00:12:43.850 --> 00:12:44.850
“Ɣ6-2?”

00:12:44.850 --> 00:12:46.140
“Pressure is holding.

00:12:46.140 --> 00:12:47.290
All good.”

00:12:47.290 --> 00:12:48.290
“Ɣ6-3?”

00:12:48.290 --> 00:12:49.290
“All clear captain.”

00:12:49.290 --> 00:12:50.290
“Ɣ6-4?”

00:12:50.290 --> 00:12:52.160
There was a silence.

00:12:52.160 --> 00:12:53.310
“Ɣ6-4?

00:12:53.310 --> 00:12:54.310
Do you copy?”

00:12:54.310 --> 00:12:55.310
“..... All clear.

00:12:55.310 --> 00:12:57.550
Sorry, doc my mic was muted.

00:12:57.550 --> 00:12:59.540
On route and ready to do this thing.”

00:12:59.540 --> 00:13:04.380
Dr. Hart sighed in relief and slowly reclined
in his chair, watching the pods move closer

00:13:04.380 --> 00:13:06.620
to the large openings on SCP-169’s side.

00:13:06.620 --> 00:13:13.370
One by one the robotic arms used underwater
torches to drill into the thick rock-like

00:13:13.370 --> 00:13:19.030
exoskeleton of the creature, screwing in complex
million-dollar equipment that was both waterproof

00:13:19.030 --> 00:13:23.060
and could withstand the immense pressure at
such a depth under the ocean.

00:13:23.060 --> 00:13:28.070
As the minutes turned to hours, Dr. Hart couldn’t
help but feel anxiety for the safety of his

00:13:28.070 --> 00:13:30.450
crew and the success of the mission.

00:13:30.450 --> 00:13:35.630
But before long the pods began to return one
by one to the mothercraft, each completing

00:13:35.630 --> 00:13:38.910
the segment of work with which they were tasked.

00:13:38.910 --> 00:13:43.800
The last pod still working was Ɣ6-1, whose
robotic arm was in the process of rotating

00:13:43.800 --> 00:13:46.450
a large industrial-sized screw.

00:13:46.450 --> 00:13:51.880
Ɣ6-1 had all but finished when suddenly a
low rumble could be felt shaking the larger

00:13:51.880 --> 00:13:52.880
submersible.

00:13:52.880 --> 00:13:54.390
Dr. Hart’s voice came crackling over the
radio.

00:13:54.390 --> 00:13:56.250
“Get back to the ship now.”

00:13:56.250 --> 00:14:00.291
Ɣ6-1, wrapped in deep focus on his task,
replied, “I’m just about finished, just

00:14:00.291 --> 00:14:01.330
packing up my tool belt.”

00:14:01.330 --> 00:14:02.460
“Leave it.

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:03.480
Return to the ship.

00:14:03.480 --> 00:14:04.480
Now.

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:05.480
That’s an order.”

00:14:05.480 --> 00:14:06.480
“Jesus, what's wrong?

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:10.160
It’s not like this is the end of the world,”
said the Agent, chuckling over his radio.

00:14:10.160 --> 00:14:15.050
An ear-piercing echo sent shockwaves through
the depths of the ocean around the submersible.

00:14:15.050 --> 00:14:21.720
The waves rumbled with the sound of SCP-169’s
voice, similar to the sound of a large whale,

00:14:21.720 --> 00:14:23.450
but amplified by a million.

00:14:23.450 --> 00:14:27.530
The sound sent all the crew members falling
to the floor as the ship experienced severe

00:14:27.530 --> 00:14:32.090
damage from the burst of pressure, slightly
cracking the glass viewing port and sending

00:14:32.090 --> 00:14:34.710
smoke flooding into the small bridge of the
ship.

00:14:34.710 --> 00:14:38.030
“Gas masks!”, shouted Dr. Hart as they
all donned the breathing apparatus.

00:14:38.030 --> 00:14:39.780
“What was that!?” asked a member of the
crew, standing shakily to his feet.

00:14:39.780 --> 00:14:41.010
“SCP-169 is waking up.

00:14:41.010 --> 00:14:43.180
Begin activation of the chemical emission
machines!

00:14:43.180 --> 00:14:45.180
We need to sedate it now!

00:14:45.180 --> 00:14:46.700
The leviathan is waking up!

00:14:46.700 --> 00:14:48.380
We need to stop it!”

00:14:48.380 --> 00:14:49.540
shouted Dr. Hart.

00:14:49.540 --> 00:14:53.150
Just then he looked back and noticed Ɣ6-1’s
pod was gone.

00:14:53.150 --> 00:14:55.640
“Ɣ6-1, what is your position?

00:14:55.640 --> 00:14:57.750
Ɣ6-1, where are you!?”

00:14:57.750 --> 00:15:00.570
“Sir, sonar has him drifting off deeper
into the ocean behind us.

00:15:00.570 --> 00:15:04.120
He must have gotten knocked off SCP-169 by
the shockwave.”

00:15:04.120 --> 00:15:05.760
But Dr. Hart wouldn’t have it.

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:08.180
He didn’t want any more blood on his hands.

00:15:08.180 --> 00:15:09.190
“Reverse thrusters!

00:15:09.190 --> 00:15:10.230
Turn us around and get us to him.

00:15:10.230 --> 00:15:11.470
We're not losing anyone!”

00:15:11.470 --> 00:15:13.060
“But the breach in the hull!”

00:15:13.060 --> 00:15:14.060
“It’ll hold.

00:15:14.060 --> 00:15:15.390
That’s what the runes are for.”

00:15:15.390 --> 00:15:19.480
The outside of the submersible began to glow
a slight blue as the ship’s systems started

00:15:19.480 --> 00:15:24.670
to come back online to full capacity and alarm
systems started to turn off and report normal

00:15:24.670 --> 00:15:26.290
pressure readings.

00:15:26.290 --> 00:15:31.540
Before long the Nemo-1 had caught up to Ɣ6-1’s
pod and retrieved the agent who had been knocked

00:15:31.540 --> 00:15:33.670
unconscious by the shock wave.

00:15:33.670 --> 00:15:37.360
Once the agent was back on the main ship,
the doctor turned his attention back to the

00:15:37.360 --> 00:15:42.430
monitors making sure the installations they
drilled into the creature were functional.

00:15:42.430 --> 00:15:48.220
Slowly the machines came online to full power,
and the speed at which SCP-169 had been moving

00:15:48.220 --> 00:15:50.130
began to slow ever so slightly.

00:15:50.130 --> 00:15:56.030
The team all watched the viewing port in silence
as a steady stream of anesthetic gas was pumping

00:15:56.030 --> 00:16:00.230
into the respiratory system of the gigantic
living myth in front of them.

00:16:00.230 --> 00:16:02.850
After a few moments of waiting, the doctor
spoke.

00:16:02.850 --> 00:16:05.500
“I think…it worked,” he said with a
smile.

00:16:05.500 --> 00:16:10.250
The crew erupted in cheers as they radioed
control back up on land that the mission had

00:16:10.250 --> 00:16:11.850
been a success.

00:16:11.850 --> 00:16:16.440
The message quickly reached the O5 Council,
where a red alert status was de-escalated

00:16:16.440 --> 00:16:20.640
and the O5 members withdrew from their plan
of leaving the current Earth for that of an

00:16:20.640 --> 00:16:21.920
alternate universe.

00:16:21.920 --> 00:16:26.790
The whole crew began to sing and celebrate
the prevention of the end of the world as

00:16:26.790 --> 00:16:31.470
Dr. Hart simply stood in front of the massive
viewport watching the mountainous specimen

00:16:31.470 --> 00:16:36.880
slowly grow smaller in the distance as the
submersible began ascending back to the world

00:16:36.880 --> 00:16:37.880
above.

00:16:37.880 --> 00:16:41.000
Ɣ6-1 came over to congratulate him, patting
him on the back.

00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:42.740
“We did it!”, he said.

00:16:42.740 --> 00:16:43.740
“Loosen up!”

00:16:43.740 --> 00:16:45.810
The doctor managed to laugh along in acknowledgment.

00:16:45.810 --> 00:16:48.960
“That we did,” he said in relief.

00:16:48.960 --> 00:16:50.320
“That we did...”

00:16:50.320 --> 00:16:55.400
The two watched the deep blue ocean in silence,
taking in the vastness of the sea.

00:16:55.400 --> 00:17:00.710
Perhaps this would not be the end of it, but
that day, they had done what the SCP Foundation

00:17:00.710 --> 00:17:05.420
did best: Kick that apocalyptic can a few
miles down the road.

00:17:05.420 --> 00:17:10.550
And sometimes, in the face of the terrifying
and the infinite, that’s really the best

00:17:10.550 --> 00:17:11.550
you can do.

00:17:11.550 --> 00:17:18.329
Now go check out “SCP-3000 - Anantashesha”
and “SCP-2316 - The Bodies in the Water”

00:17:18.329 --> 00:17:20.730
for more frightening aquatic anomalies!

